          "text": "A CUDA graph replay is faster than eager kernel launches primarily because it eliminates the CPU-side overhead associated with the CUDA driver and runtime. When you launch kernels eagerly, the CPU must perform a significant amount of work for every single kernel: it validates parameters, allocates resources, and communicates with the driver to schedule the work. This creates a bottleneck where the GPU often finishes its current task and sits idle while waiting for the CPU to prepare and dispatch the next kernel. By capturing a sequence of operations into a graph, you effectively \"pre-compile\" this sequence. On replay, the CPU simply issues a single command to execute the entire graph, allowing the GPU to transition between kernels with minimal latency and keeping the hardware fully saturated.\n\nThe cost of this performance gain is a loss of flexibility and increased complexity in code management. Graphs require a strict definition of the execution flow upfront; you must capture the exact sequence of kernels, memory copies, and dependencies before execution begins. This makes it difficult to implement dynamic logic where the number of kernels or their parameters might change based on runtime data (unless you use conditional nodes or multiple graphs). Additionally, there is a memory cost, as the graph structure itself must be stored in device memory. Finally, the development process becomes more complex, as you",
